<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.12.3: Making Predictions Using a Quadratic Model</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02294</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.12.3: Making Predictions Using a Quadratic Model</md:title>
    <md:uuid>23ef286d-6afd-4f74-a96a-5009f545ab5d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>A helium balloon is released and floats up before coming back down to the ground.</para>
<para>The following data set shows the height in feet, \(y\), of the balloon corresponding to the number of minutes passed, \(x\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        \(x_1\) (minutes)
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> \(y_1\) (height in feet) </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> 1 </entry>
      <entry> 99.7 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 1.5 </entry>
      <entry> 130 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 3 </entry>
      <entry> 149 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 4.5 </entry>
      <entry> 172 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 7 </entry>
      <entry> 108 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> 9 </entry>
      <entry> 84 </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the Desmos below. Click the + symbol in the top left and select a table. Enter the data set into the table.</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-bottom="-20" data-height="500" data-left="-5" data-right="20" data-schema-version="1.0" data-top="150" data-width="600"/>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Use the instruction line, \(y_1 \sim ax_1^2+bx_1+ c\), to find the curve of best fit.</para>
    <para>What is the equation of the curve of best fit? Round coefficients to the nearest hundredth.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the equation of the curve of best fit.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(y = -3.97x^2+35.22x + 78.63\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How high will the balloon be after 4 minutes? (Hint: Graph the line \(x = 4\) to help identify where it intersects with the curve of best fit.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the height of the balloon after 4 minutes.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Approximately 156.0 feet</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How high will the balloon be after 10 minutes?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the height of the balloon after 10 minutes.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Approximately 33.83 feet</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>At what time will the balloon first be at a height of 95 feet? (Graph the line \(y = 95\).)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the time the balloon will first be at a height of 95 feet.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>At approximately 0.49 minute</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>At what time will the balloon return to a height of 95 feet?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the time the balloon will return to a height of 95 feet.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> At approximately 8.38 minutes</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>When will the balloon reach the ground, or have a height of 0 feet?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the time the balloon will reach the ground.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>At approximately 10.72 minutes</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

